Found while writing ADR 0017 (Response.created becomes a Langertha::Moment).

lib/Langertha/RateLimit.pm:70 and :107 declare requests_reset / tokens_reset as Maybe[Str],
and their own POD says the format "varies by provider (seconds, RFC 3339 timestamp, or epoch)".
That is the same untyped-wire-value problem Response.created had before ADR 0017 -- one
attribute holding whatever the provider header said -- except that here the shapes are not all
the same KIND of value: "seconds" is a duration, RFC3339 and epoch are instants.

So Langertha::Moment is NOT a straight drop-in: from_wire would happily turn the bare
duration "60" into an instant at 1970-01-01T00:01:00Z, which is wrong, not merely lossy.
Any fix has to decide first whether the attribute means "when" or "in how long", and
probably split it (reset_after_seconds vs reset_at) rather than widen the type.

Not urgent: unlike GH #3 nothing croaks today, because Maybe[Str] accepts every shape. The
cost is on consumers, who cannot tell which shape they got without knowing the provider.

Deliberately NOT bundled into the created/Time::Moment change -- different question, and it
touches the rate-limit header readers of every engine family.

Reviewed 2026-08-31 (weak-feel): stays in backlog, needs a maintainer design decision before code.
Nothing croaks today (Maybe[Str] accepts every shape), so this is not a latent bug like GH #3 was.
The fix is not a type-widen: the attribute conflates a duration ("in how long", seconds) with an
instant ("when", RFC3339/epoch), so Langertha::Moment->from_wire would turn a bare "60" into
1970-01-01T00:01:00Z -- wrong, not merely lossy. The real fix is an API split (reset_after_seconds
vs reset_at) that touches the rate-limit header readers of every engine family (OpenAICompatible
x-ratelimit-*, AnthropicCompatible anthropic-ratelimit-*). That is a product/API decision, not a
worker call -- deferring rather than guessing a new public shape. Possibly langertha-llm-advisor.


