`Retry-After` (RFC 9110 10.2.3) is `HTTP-date / delay-seconds`; every LLM provider that documents
it uses delay-seconds only. Langertha reads it nowhere.

The IETF draft (`draft-ietf-httpapi-ratelimit-headers`, rev -11, May 2026) is NOT what any of
these implement, and it has moved on from the shape people remember: it no longer defines
`RateLimit-Limit`/`-Remaining`/`-Reset` at all, but two Structured-Field headers
(`RateLimit-Policy: "fixedwindow";q=100;w=60` and `RateLimit: "fixedwindow";r=99;t=50`) whose
`w`/`t` are INTEGER SECONDS with sub-second precision explicitly forbidden. Do not design toward
it; nothing in this market speaks it.

## Recommended API

Four points. The first is a bug fix the ticket did not know about; the rest are the split.

### 1. Fix `raw` first -- it is not what its POD claims

`Langertha::RateLimit::raw`'s POD says "HashRef of all rate-limit-related headers as returned by
the provider". It is not: both readers enumerate a fixed list
(`Role::OpenAICompatible::_parse_rate_limit_headers`, six names;
`Role::AnthropicCompatible::_parse_rate_limit_headers`, twelve) and everything else is DISCARDED
before `raw` is built. Headers that exist today and are lost entirely:
`x-ratelimit-*-project-tokens` (OpenAI), `anthropic-priority-*` and `anthropic-fast-*`
(Anthropic), and every Mistral header (their names carry a `-minute` window suffix and match
neither list). Fix: collect every response header matching
`/^(x-ratelimit-|anthropic-ratelimit-|anthropic-priority-|anthropic-fast-|ratelimitbysize-)/i`
plus `retry-after` into `raw`, then normalize the known subset out of it. That is a strict
superset of today's behaviour and loses nothing -- and it is the precondition for the split
below, because "keep the raw header value" only means something if the header reached `raw`.

### 2. Split each bucket into an instant and a duration, both `Maybe`

    requests_reset_at        Maybe[Langertha::Moment]   # WHEN     (ADR 0017 value object)
    requests_reset_after     Maybe[Num]                 # IN HOW LONG, seconds (may be fractional)
    tokens_reset_at          Maybe[Langertha::Moment]
    tokens_reset_after       Maybe[Num]

Only the attribute the wire actually spoke is populated by the parser. Deriving the other one
needs a receive instant, so:

### 3. Add `received` (Langertha::Moment) and derive lazily, never eagerly

    received  Langertha::Moment   # stamped when the headers were parsed

`requests_reset_at` is then a lazy builder: `received + requests_reset_after` when only the
duration was sent. `requests_reset_after` is the mirror: `requests_reset_at - received` when only
the instant was sent. Both stay `undef` when neither was sent (Mistral, xAI, DeepSeek, Gemini,
Hetzner, every self-hosted server) -- absence is the EXPECTED path here, not an error, and no
default may be invented. This is the same normalized-plus-native split as ADR 0011 (`timing`) and
ADR 0017 (`created` + `raw`), applied to a third response-side surface.

`received` is worth having on its own: it is the only thing that makes an Anthropic RFC 3339
reset actionable without asking the caller to trust its own clock against Anthropic's.

### 4. Keep `requests_reset` / `tokens_reset` as they are, deprecated

They stay `Maybe[Str]` returning the verbatim header, POD'd as deprecated in favour of the pair.
Nothing that reads them breaks; the ticket's stated cost ("consumers cannot tell which shape they
got") is paid off by the new attributes, not by mutating the old ones. Removing them is a
separate, later decision.

## The parser -- three branches, and one that must not be `^\d+s$`

    _parse_reset($string) -> ( at => Moment ) | ( after => Num ) | ()

1. **Go duration** -- `/^(\d+(?:\.\d+)?(?:ns|us|\x{00b5}s|ms|s|m|h))+$/`, summed. MUST handle
   compound (`6m0s`, `2m59.56s`), sub-second (`250ms`, `35ms`, `20ms`) and fractional (`7.66s`).
   A naive `^(\d+)s$` breaks on Groq's own documented example and on every Scaleway response.
   -> `after`.
2. **RFC 3339** -- hand to `Langertha::Moment->from_wire` (ADR 0017's lenient inbound door; it
   already never dies and drops what it cannot read). -> `at`.
3. **Bare number** -- ambiguous, and the ONLY branch where guessing happens. Disambiguate by
   magnitude, document the heuristic in the POD, and keep `raw` so a caller can override:
   `>= 1e12` -> epoch milliseconds -> `at`; `>= 1e9` -> epoch seconds -> `at`; otherwise seconds
   -> `after`. This branch exists for Cerebras (undocumented fractional seconds) and possibly
   OpenRouter (unofficial epoch-ms, error responses only). Both are UNCONFIRMED against
   first-party docs -- the heuristic is a best effort over an undocumented wire, and the POD
   should say so rather than implying it is authoritative.
4. Anything else -> return nothing; `raw` still has the string. Never die on a rate-limit header.

## Two semantic traps to POD, not to normalize away

- **Groq's `x-ratelimit-*-requests` is Requests Per DAY**, not per minute -- their docs state it
  explicitly ("Always refers to Requests Per Day (RPD)") while `*-tokens` is TPM. Two buckets with
  different windows land in the same two normalized fields today. Do not try to model the window;
  just say so in the POD and leave the window visible in `raw`.
- **Cerebras puts the window in the header NAME** (`-requests-day`), so a name-list reader misses
  it entirely. Another argument for the prefix-match `raw` in point 1.

## Scope note

The split touches both readers plus `to_hash` / `TO_JSON`, and `TO_JSON` must keep excluding
`raw` (that exclusion is a deliberate privacy decision already recorded in RateLimit.pm's POD --
do not let a wider `raw` leak into traces). Nothing here changes behaviour for a caller that
reads only `requests_remaining` / `tokens_remaining`.

## Verdict

The ticket's instinct is correct and the API split is the right fix. The one thing the ticket
underestimates: `raw` does not currently hold the raw headers for anything outside the twelve
enumerated names, so "do not lose the raw value" is not yet true today and should be fixed in the
same change.
